/* Held-out question bank — reserved for the full-length practice and the
 * exam simulation. Never served by a domain drill, quick quiz or weak-area
 * drill, so a practice sitting still tests knowledge after the drill pool
 * has been memorized.
 *
 * Marked with heldOut:true; poolFor() keeps the two sets disjoint.
 *
 * Option lengths are balanced deliberately: in the drill bank the correct
 * answer was the longest option 95% of the time, which let it be picked
 * without reading. Distractors here are full, plausible claims.
 */
QUESTIONS.push(

/* ---- CCAR-F D1: Agentic Architecture & Orchestration (27%) ---- */

{ id:"hx-d1-01", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent must book travel: search flights, hold a seat, then charge a card. The hold succeeds but the charge fails. What should the design already specify?",
  a:2,
  opts:["That the agent retries the charge repeatedly until the payment provider finally accepts it","That the whole sequence restarts from the flight search again, with fresh availability","A compensating action that releases the hold, so a partial run leaves no orphaned state","That the error is returned to the user with the seat hold simply left in place for them"],
  why:"A multi-step sequence with side effects needs to define what happens when it stops halfway. Releasing the hold is the compensating action; without it the seat stays locked and nobody owns the cleanup. Retrying the charge may be right for a transient failure but does not address the partial state, and restarting from search abandons the hold rather than releasing it."},

{ id:"hx-d1-02", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What does a coordinator need from each worker for a synthesis step to be auditable?",
  a:1,
  opts:["The total number of tokens each worker consumed while running its subtask","Each finding paired with the source it came from, preserved through the merge","The order in which each of the workers completed the subtasks assigned to them","A confidence score that each worker assigns to the conclusion it has reached"],
  why:"Auditability means tracing a statement in the final answer back to what produced it, which requires claim-to-source mapping that survives synthesis. Token counts and completion order describe the run, not the reasoning. Self-reported confidence is not calibrated and cannot substitute for provenance."},

{ id:"hx-d1-03", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A long-running agent is 30 minutes into a task when the user asks it to stop. What should happen to work already completed?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Persist what finished, record where it stopped and report both to the user","Discard everything, since a partially completed task cannot be relied upon afterwards","Continue to a natural stopping point before acknowledging the cancellation","Roll back every action taken so the system returns to its original state"],
  why:"Cancellation is a defined state, not an abort: completed work usually has value and the user needs to know what did and did not happen. Discarding wastes it, continuing ignores the request, and blanket rollback is wrong where actions were legitimate and irreversible."},

{ id:"hx-d1-04", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Two agents in a pipeline each work correctly in isolation, but the combined output is inconsistent. Where do you look first?",
  a:3,
  opts:["The model tier, which may be too low to sustain quality across two stages","The temperature setting, since variance compounds across a two-stage pipeline","The prompts, which were probably written by different people to different standards","The contract between them, meaning what the first promises to emit and the second expects"],
  why:"Components that each pass in isolation but fail together point at the interface, not the components. An unstated or mismatched contract is the usual cause. Tier, temperature and prompt style would degrade each stage individually, which the premise rules out."},

{ id:"hx-d1-05", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Which conditions make a human approval gate worth its latency cost?",
  multi:true, a:[0,2,3],
  opts:["The action cannot be undone once it has been taken",
        "The agent has more than five tools available to it",
        "The action affects someone outside the organization running the agent",
        "A wrong decision is expensive relative to the cost of a human review"],
  why:"Irreversibility, external impact and an unfavorable error-to-review cost ratio are what justify blocking on a human. Tool count describes the agent's surface area, not the consequence of any particular action, and a gate on every tool-rich agent would stop useful work for no benefit."},

{ id:"hx-d1-06", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An orchestrator must choose between three workers whose capabilities overlap. What makes the routing decision reliable?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Giving the orchestrator a more capable model so it judges the overlapping cases better","Making the workers' responsibilities disjoint, so only one can apply to a request","Asking each worker whether it can handle the request before assigning it","Routing to all three and selecting whichever response arrives first"],
  why:"Reliable routing comes from removing the ambiguity rather than reasoning through it. If two workers can both plausibly serve a request, the overlap is the defect. Polling each worker triples the latency, and racing them wastes work and picks on speed rather than fit."},

{ id:"hx-d1-07", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A nightly agent job succeeds most nights but occasionally produces a truncated report. What is the most likely cause to rule out first?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The scheduler is starting the job before its input data has finished loading overnight","The model version changed between runs, altering how much it generates","A run occasionally hits the output ceiling and the loop treats it as finished","Network instability is dropping the connection partway through the response"],
  why:"Intermittent truncation with no error is the signature of an unhandled stop reason: the output ceiling is reached, generation halts mid-way, and a loop that does not branch on it accepts the partial result as complete. The other causes would usually surface as an error or a consistent failure rather than silent truncation."},

{ id:"hx-d1-08", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What distinguishes a tool from a subagent as a unit of delegation?",
  a:0,
  opts:["A tool performs one defined operation, while a subagent reasons over a goal with its own context","A tool runs on your own infrastructure, while a subagent runs on the model provider's own","A tool returns structured data, while a subagent can only ever return free-form prose back","A tool is invoked directly by your code, while a subagent is invoked by the model itself"],
  why:"The distinction is scope of judgment: a tool executes a specified operation, a subagent is given an objective and decides how to pursue it within its own context. Both run on your side, both can return structured results, and both are requested by the model."},

{ id:"hx-d1-09", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Your agent's cost per run varies by a factor of ten with no visible difference in output quality. What should you investigate?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Whether the model tier is being selected correctly for each request type","Whether prompt caching is enabled on the stable portion of the request","Whether the output ceiling is set high enough for the longest answer the task can produce","How many iterations each run takes, and whether some are looping without progress"],
  why:"A tenfold spread with equivalent output points at iteration count rather than per-call cost: some runs are going round the loop far more than others without improving the result. Tier, caching and the output ceiling shift cost by a consistent factor rather than producing that kind of variance."},

{ id:"hx-d1-10", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A workflow's third step fails for 2% of inputs. The first two steps have already written to external systems. What does the design need?",
  a:1,
  opts:["A retry on the third step, so the transient 2% resolves on a second attempt","A defined recovery path: either compensate the earlier writes or complete them later","Validation before the first step, so inputs that would fail never enter the workflow at all","A larger model for the third step, which is evidently the hardest of the three"],
  why:"Partial completion with external side effects needs an explicit answer for the 2%: undo what was written, or queue the remainder for completion. A retry helps only if the failure is transient, upfront validation cannot catch every case, and model capability does not address the orphaned state."},

{ id:"hx-d1-11", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Why does an agentic design need a budget as well as an iteration cap?",
  a:2,
  opts:["Because the iteration cap is only ever advisory and the model can simply run past it anyway","Because a budget is far easier for stakeholders to reason about than a count of iterations","Because a single iteration can be arbitrarily expensive, so counting them bounds the wrong thing","Because the API enforces a spend limit on you but does not enforce any iteration limit"],
  why:"Iterations and cost are only loosely related: one turn that reads a large document can cost more than ten small ones, so a cap on count leaves spend unbounded. Both limits are enforced by your code, and both are worth having for different reasons."},

{ id:"hx-d1-12", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent's output is correct but takes 40 seconds, and users abandon it after 10. What should be examined before adding capacity?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Whether anything can be shown progressively, so the wait is occupied rather than blank","Whether a faster tier completes the same reasoning, since the budget is the constraint","Whether the output ceiling can drop, since generation then finishes inside the window","Whether the task can be rejected early, since a long one is identifiable up front"],
  why:"Abandonment is about perceived wait, not total time, so streaming or staged output often fixes it without touching the work. A faster tier may lose quality, lowering the ceiling truncates the answer, and rejecting the task refuses the request rather than serving it."},

{ id:"hx-d1-13", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A subagent returns a result the orchestrator did not expect but which is clearly useful. What does this suggest about the design?",
  a:1,
  opts:["The subagent is exceeding its brief and its permissions should be narrowed",
        "The contract is underspecified: the orchestrator should define what it will accept",
        "The orchestrator's model tier is too low to anticipate the subagent's output",
        "The subagent should be merged into the orchestrator to remove the interface"],
  why:"An unexpected-but-useful result means the interface was never pinned down, so neither side can rely on what the other produces. Tightening the contract is the fix. Narrowing permissions punishes useful behavior, and merging removes the separation that made the subagent worthwhile."},

{ id:"hx-d1-14", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Which failure is the parallelization pattern specifically vulnerable to?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Each branch producing its output in a slightly different format from all of the others","The total cost scaling directly with the number of branches that end up being run","One branch finishing a great deal sooner than the rest and then sitting idle","Branches silently depending on each other, so running them concurrently changes the result"],
  why:"Parallelization is only correct when the branches are genuinely independent. A hidden dependency produces results that differ from the sequential case, often intermittently. Format drift and cost scaling are real but affect any fan-out design, and an idle branch is not a correctness problem."},

{ id:"hx-d1-15", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent repeatedly asks the user for information it was already given earlier in the session. What is the architectural cause?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The model tier is too low to retain all of those details across a long conversation","The temperature is high enough that the agent forgets much of its earlier context","Facts that must persist are living only in the transcript, which has been compacted away","The user's answers are being appended as assistant turns rather than as user ones"],
  why:"Anything that must survive compaction has to be held outside the transcript and re-supplied deliberately. If it lives only in conversation history it disappears when that history is summarized. Tier and temperature do not govern what is retained, and a role mix-up would produce different symptoms."},

{ id:"hx-d1-16", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"You are asked to add 'self-healing' to an agent so it recovers from any error without human involvement. How should you respond?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Agree, and implement a catch-all retry wrapper around every tool invocation",
        "Define which errors are genuinely recoverable and escalate the rest rather than looping",
        "Decline, since automated recovery conflicts with having an audit trail",
        "Agree, and raise the iteration cap so the agent has room to work through failures"],
  why:"Not every error has a recovery: a permission failure or a malformed request will not clear on retry. Classifying them and escalating the rest is what makes recovery meaningful. A catch-all wrapper and a higher cap both turn permanent failures into expensive loops."},

{ id:"hx-d1-17", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What is the practical consequence of the API being stateless for agent design?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Everything the model needs to continue must be in the request you send",
        "Each request must be authenticated separately with a fresh credential",
        "Tool results cannot be carried across more than one turn of a conversation",
        "The conversation must be rebuilt from storage before every single request"],
  why:"Statelessness means the server holds nothing between calls, so the full context travels with each request. Credentials are reused, tool results persist as long as you keep sending them, and rebuilding from storage is one implementation choice rather than a requirement."},

{ id:"hx-d1-18", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A review finds that an agent's behavior cannot be explained after the fact. What is the minimum the design must add?",
  a:2,
  opts:["A summary the agent writes itself, covering what it did, why, and what it skipped","A higher log level, capturing stdout, stderr, and the timings during the whole run","A record of each request sent, each tool called with its arguments, and each result","A replay mode, re-running the same input, the parameters, and the tool versions"],
  why:"Explaining a past decision requires what the model actually saw and what each tool returned. A self-authored summary is the model's account rather than evidence, raw logs lack the sequence, and replay produces a fresh run rather than reconstructing the original."},

{ id:"hx-d1-19", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d1", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent that worked well in testing performs poorly on real traffic. Nothing in the system changed. What is the most likely explanation?",
  a:3,
  opts:["The model provider silently updated the version the agent is pinned to",
        "Production latency is causing timeouts that testing never encountered",
        "Concurrency in production is exhausting the rate limit available to the agent",
        "The test inputs were not representative of what real users actually send"],
  why:"A gap between test and production performance with no system change usually means the test set was unrepresentative — cleaner, shorter or narrower than reality. The other causes are real but would show as errors or latency rather than as a quality drop."},

/* ---- CCAR-F D2: Tool Design & MCP Integration (18%) ---- */

{ id:"hx-d2-01", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A tool returns a list of records, and the agent frequently needs only one field from each. What should change?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Instruct the model to request only the field it needs in the tool arguments",
        "Let the caller specify which fields to return, and default to a minimal set",
        "Return everything, since the model can disregard the fields it does not use",
        "Split the tool into one tool per field so each returns exactly one value"],
  why:"Field selection with a lean default keeps the payload proportionate to the need without losing access to the rest. The model cannot ignore tokens it has already been made to read, and a tool per field multiplies the catalog and the selection problem."},

{ id:"hx-d2-02", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What belongs in a tool's description that does not belong in its input schema?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The data types each parameter accepts and whether it is required",
        "The permitted range of values for any numeric or enumerated parameter",
        "When the tool should be used, and when a different tool is the better choice",
        "The structure of the object the tool returns when the call succeeds"],
  why:"The schema constrains the shape of a call; the description governs whether the call is made at all. Selection guidance — including when not to use it — has nowhere else to live. Types, ranges and required fields are schema concerns."},

{ id:"hx-d2-03", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An MCP server exposes a tool that takes 90 seconds to complete. What should the integration do?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Return a handle immediately and let the agent poll, rather than blocking the loop","Raise the client timeout so the call has room to finish before it is abandoned as failed","Split the operation into shorter tools the agent calls one after another","Cache the result so that only the first call in a session pays the full cost"],
  why:"A tool call that outlives a reasonable turn should not hold the loop open. Returning a handle makes the wait explicit and cancellable. A longer timeout defers the problem, splitting may not be possible, and caching does nothing for the first call or for varying inputs."},

{ id:"hx-d2-04", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Two teams each run an MCP server exposing a tool with the same name but different behavior. What is the risk?",
  a:3,
  opts:["The client will reject the second server because the name already exists",
        "The model will call both tools and reconcile whichever result it prefers",
        "Only the first server registered will have its version of the tool available",
        "The agent may call one while the prompt and the operator assume the other"],
  why:"Name collisions across servers are a correctness hazard: the agent resolves to one implementation while everyone reasoning about the system assumes the other. Namespacing tool names by service prevents it. Nothing automatically rejects, merges or hides the duplicate."},

{ id:"hx-d2-05", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Which practices keep a tool catalog maintainable as it grows?",
  multi:true, a:[0,1,3],
  opts:["Namespacing tool names by the service or resource they act on",
        "Consolidating related operations behind one tool with an action parameter",
        "Keeping every tool available on every request for consistency",
        "Removing tools that are no longer called rather than leaving them defined"],
  why:"Namespacing, consolidation and pruning all reduce the choice set the model must reason about. Keeping everything available on every request does the opposite: it degrades selection accuracy and spends context on definitions that will not be used."},

{ id:"hx-d2-06", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A tool's result is correct but the agent misinterprets it, treating an empty list as an error. What is the fix?",
  a:2,
  opts:["Return an error flag when the list is empty so the agent handles it explicitly",
        "Instruct the agent in the system prompt that an empty list means no matches",
        "Return a result that states the outcome, such as a count alongside the list",
        "Return null instead of an empty list so the distinction is unambiguous"],
  why:"A result the model can misread should be made self-describing: a count or an explicit status removes the ambiguity at the source. Flagging a successful search as an error teaches the agent to retry valid results, prompt instructions are advisory, and null is no clearer than an empty list."},

{ id:"hx-d2-07", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Your agent calls a tool that occasionally returns stale data from a cache. How should the tool communicate this?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Omit cached results entirely so the agent only ever sees current data",
        "Include the age of the data in the result so the agent can judge its relevance",
        "Return an error when the cached entry is older than a defined threshold",
        "Refresh the cache on every call so staleness never arises in practice"],
  why:"Freshness is context the model can reason with if you give it. Suppressing cached results discards usable data, an error misrepresents a successful call, and refreshing every time abandons the reason for caching."},

{ id:"hx-d2-08", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What does MCP's client-server split mean for where you enforce authorization?",
  a:3,
  opts:["In the tool description, which tells the model what it is and is not allowed to do","In the client, which decides which of the server's tools to expose","In the model's system prompt, which sets the boundaries of the session","On the server, which executes the operation and must check it independently"],
  why:"The server runs the code, so it is the only place a check cannot be bypassed. Client-side exposure and prompt instructions shape what is likely to be attempted, not what is possible, and a description is read by the model rather than enforced."},

{ id:"hx-d2-09", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A tool needs a parameter the user has not supplied and the model cannot infer. What should the tool do?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Return a structured error naming the missing parameter so the agent can ask for it",
        "Substitute a sensible default and note the substitution in the result",
        "Fail silently and return an empty result, letting the agent try a different approach",
        "Infer the most likely value from the conversation and proceed with the call"],
  why:"A missing required input is a recoverable condition if the tool says exactly what is missing — the agent can then ask the user. A silent default or inference produces a confident answer to the wrong question, and an empty result hides the cause."},

{ id:"hx-d2-10", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent is given a tool that can delete records, but the use case only ever reads them. What is the correct design?",
  a:2,
  opts:["Keep the tool and add a confirmation prompt before any delete is executed",
        "Keep the tool but instruct the agent never to call the delete operation",
        "Do not expose the delete capability to this agent at all",
        "Keep the tool and log every delete so misuse can be identified afterwards"],
  why:"Capability the use case does not need should not be present: removal is the only control that cannot be talked around. Confirmation and logging are compensating and detective controls, useful where the capability is genuinely required, and an instruction is advisory."},

{ id:"hx-d2-11", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Why are parallel tool calls returned in a single user message rather than several?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Because the API processes a single message rather faster than several in sequence","Because the assistant turn requesting them is one turn, and its results answer it as one","Because splitting them up would exceed the maximum number of messages allowed","Because the model cannot correlate results that arrive in separate user messages"],
  why:"The conversation alternates turns: one assistant turn containing several tool_use blocks is answered by one user turn containing the matching tool_result blocks. Splitting breaks that structure and is rejected. It is a protocol requirement rather than a performance optimization."},

{ id:"hx-d2-12", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A tool integration works in development but fails in production with permission errors. What is the most likely cause?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Production traffic volume is exceeding the rate limit set on the downstream service","The tool definition differs between the two environments after a partial deployment","The production model version is interpreting the tool description rather differently","The service account used in production is scoped more narrowly than the development one"],
  why:"Permission errors specifically point at the identity making the call. Development credentials are often broader, so an operation that worked locally is refused in production. Rate limits surface as 429s, and tool definitions and model versions do not produce permission failures."},

{ id:"hx-d2-13", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d2", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"You need to deprecate a tool that several agents still call. What is the safest sequence?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Mark it deprecated in its description, migrate callers, then remove it once unused","Remove it immediately, break all of the callers, and let the migration follow after","Leave it in place indefinitely, stop documenting it at all, and let the use decay","Replace its implementation, return an error from it, and explain the change there"],
  why:"Signalling first, migrating, then removing keeps every caller working through the transition. Immediate removal and an error-returning stub both break callers before they have moved, and leaving it undocumented keeps the maintenance burden while losing the guidance."},

/* ---- CCAR-F D3: Claude Code Configuration & Workflows (20%) ---- */

{ id:"hx-d3-01", scn:["codegen"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A team wants every agent session to start knowing the repository's architecture. Where does that belong?",
  a:2,
  opts:["In a slash command each developer runs at the beginning of their session",
        "In each developer's personal configuration, copied from a shared template",
        "In committed project memory, which loads automatically with the checkout",
        "In the system prompt of the application that invokes the agent"],
  why:"Committed project memory travels with the repository, so a fresh clone has it and an improvement reaches everyone. A command must be remembered, personal config must be set up per person, and the application system prompt is not how a coding session gets project context."},

{ id:"hx-d3-02", scn:["devprod"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What does a SessionStart hook let you do that a memory file cannot?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Guarantee the instructions are followed, since a hook runs before the model reads them","Run code at session start, so the context injected can be computed rather than static","Override a higher-precedence layer, since a hook's output is applied last in the session","Prevent the session starting, since a failing hook halts startup before any turn begins"],
  why:"A hook executes, so it can inject context derived from the current state — the branch, open tickets, recent failures — which a static file cannot express. It does not make its output binding, and it supplies context rather than gating the session."},

{ id:"hx-d3-03", scn:["codegen"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A developer reports that the agent edits files the team agreed were off limits. The rule is documented in project memory. What went wrong?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Memory is guidance, so the restriction needs a deny permission rule or a hook",
        "The memory file is too long and the rule is being lost among the other content",
        "The rule is phrased ambiguously and needs to name the paths more precisely",
        "The developer's session loaded an older copy of the memory file from cache"],
  why:"A documented rule is advisory however it is worded. Enforcement requires a mechanism the model cannot choose to ignore. Length and phrasing affect how often guidance is followed, not whether it can be overridden."},

{ id:"hx-d3-04", scn:["ci"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"You want a code review to run automatically on every pull request using the agent. What makes this reliable in CI?",
  a:3,
  opts:["A generous timeout, so that the review completes even on an unusually large diff","A retry step, so that a transient failure does not end up blocking the pull request","Verbose output, so that a human reviewer can see what the agent actually considered","Non-interactive mode with permissions resolved in advance and a checked exit status"],
  why:"An automated run must never wait for input and must report its outcome in a form the pipeline acts on. Timeouts, retries and verbosity are useful, but a run that stops at a permission prompt or whose status is never checked fails regardless."},

{ id:"hx-d3-05", scn:["devprod"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What is the relationship between a slash command and a skill?",
  a:2,
  opts:["A slash command is a skill that has been given a shorter invocation name","A skill is a slash command that the model may invoke without being asked","A command runs on explicit invocation, while a skill is guidance the model may draw on","They are the same mechanism, with the name differing by where the definition is stored"],
  why:"The difference is who initiates: a command is something you invoke, a skill is reusable instruction the model loads when it judges it relevant. They are distinct mechanisms rather than two names for one thing."},

{ id:"hx-d3-06", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent keeps running a slow full test suite when a targeted run would do. Where should the guidance live?",
  a:2,
  opts:["In a hook that intercepts the test command and rewrites it to a narrower scope",
        "In a slash command the developer invokes when they want the fast path",
        "In project memory, stating which suite to run for which kind of change",
        "In a permission rule that denies the full suite outside continuous integration"],
  why:"This is guidance about judgment, not a rule that must hold, so project memory is the right weight. A hook rewriting commands is surprising and hides what actually ran, a command relies on the developer remembering, and denying the full suite blocks a legitimate action."},

{ id:"hx-d3-07", scn:["devprod"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent session is producing good code but ignoring the project's error-handling conventions. The conventions are in a skill. Why might it not apply?",
  a:0,
  opts:["A skill is loaded when the model judges it relevant, and it may not have","Skills apply only to new files, not to edits of existing ones","Skills are overridden by any convention already present in the surrounding code","A skill must be invoked explicitly before it affects the session"],
  why:"Skills are drawn on at the model's discretion, so relevance is a judgment that can go the wrong way. If a convention must always apply, project memory or enforcement is the right home. Skills are not restricted to new files and do not need explicit invocation."},

{ id:"hx-d3-08", scn:["codegen"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Which layer should hold a rule that must survive a developer editing the repository?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Project configuration, committed so that everyone receives it on checkout",
        "A PreToolUse hook defined in the repository's configuration directory",
        "A path-scoped rule placed in the directory the restriction applies to",
        "The managed layer, which a developer cannot edit from inside the project"],
  why:"Anything in the repository is editable by anyone with commit access, including hooks and scoped rules. A rule that must hold against a determined developer belongs in the managed layer, which sits above project configuration in precedence and outside their control."},

{ id:"hx-d3-09", scn:["codegen","devprod"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A PreToolUse hook is intended to block commits that fail lint, but commits still go through. The script runs and detects failures correctly. What is wrong?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The hook is registered for the wrong tool, so its exit 2 never reaches the commit call","The hook writes its verdict to stdout, and only stderr is read when the code is 1","The hook exits with a non-zero code other than 2, which does not block","The hook sits at a scope the session does not load, so nothing runs and 0 is assumed"],
  why:"Only exit code 2 blocks: every other non-zero code is treated as a non-blocking error, so a hook that exits 1 detects correctly, logs correctly and stops nothing. The premise states the script runs and detects, which rules out registration and scope."},

{ id:"hx-d3-10", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A long-running agent session is approaching a deadline and you want to hand the work to a colleague. What transfers the state?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Committed artefacts plus a written summary of decisions, since session state is local","The session identifier, since your colleague can resume it on their own machine","An export of the conversation, since replaying it rebuilds exactly the same context","A fork of the session, since that branches the work out to a second participant"],
  why:"Session state belongs to the machine and the account that created it, so handover happens through the repository and a human summary. Resumption and forking both continue work within the original environment rather than moving it, and replaying a transcript does not reproduce the working tree."},

{ id:"hx-d3-11", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What does forking a session give you that resuming does not?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Two independent branches from one baseline, neither affecting the other",
        "A fresh context window with the previous conversation summarized into it",
        "The ability to continue work after the original process has exited",
        "A record of the session that can be replayed for audit purposes"],
  why:"Forking creates divergent branches sharing a common prefix, so two approaches can be explored without contaminating each other. Resumption continues one line of work. Neither summarizes automatically, and neither is an audit mechanism."},

{ id:"hx-d3-12", scn:["ci"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A headless run returns a non-zero exit code, but the pipeline reports success. What is the defect?",
  a:3,
  opts:["The run emitted its result to stderr, which the pipeline does not capture",
        "The output format was not machine-parseable, so the result could not be read",
        "The agent completed its work, so the non-zero code was a spurious warning",
        "The pipeline step does not check the exit status of the command it runs"],
  why:"An exit code only matters if something acts on it. A step that ignores the status reports success regardless. The other options describe real problems but none of them would turn a non-zero exit into a reported success."},

{ id:"hx-d3-13", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Two developers get different agent behavior on the same repository and the same task. What should be compared first?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The model version each session is pinned to in its configuration",
        "The phrasing each developer used when describing the task",
        "The full set of configuration each session actually loaded, and in what order",
        "Whether either developer has uncommitted changes in their working tree"],
  why:"Inconsistent behavior on identical input almost always traces to inconsistent configuration, and the loaded set is cheap to compare. Model version is part of that comparison. Prompt phrasing and working-tree state matter but come after the configuration is ruled out."},

{ id:"hx-d3-14", scn:["research"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d3", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Why does a subagent definition declare its own tool access rather than inheriting the parent's?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Because tool definitions simply cannot be shared across two entirely separate context windows","So each subagent holds only the capability its task requires, limiting what a mistake reaches","Because the parent's own tools may not even be available in the subagent's environment","To cut down the token cost of sending the entire tool catalog to every subagent spawned"],
  why:"Declaring access per subagent is least privilege applied to delegation: a research subagent does not need write tools, so a wrong decision cannot reach them. Token cost is a side benefit rather than the reason, and inheritance is technically possible — it is simply the wrong default."},

/* ---- CCAR-F D4: Prompt Engineering & Structured Output (20%) ---- */

{ id:"hx-d4-01", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A classification prompt is right 90% of the time, and the errors cluster on one ambiguous category. What is the most direct fix?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Add examples covering that category's boundary, including the near-miss cases","Raise the model tier, so that the ambiguity gets resolved rather more reliably","Lower the temperature, so that the classification becomes far more deterministic","Split the prompt into one call per category and then combine all of the verdicts"],
  why:"Errors concentrated at one boundary mean the criterion for that category is underspecified, and worked examples of the edge cases communicate it better than description. A stronger model may help but does not supply the missing definition, and the other changes address variance rather than ambiguity."},

{ id:"hx-d4-02", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What does a prefill give you, and what does it not guarantee?",
  a:2,
  opts:["It guarantees valid JSON, but not that the values will be semantically correct",
        "It guarantees the response length, but not the structure of what is produced",
        "It fixes how the response starts, but the model may still diverge after that",
        "It guarantees the model will call a tool, but not which tool it selects"],
  why:"Prefill constrains the opening of the response and nothing beyond it, so the model can still drift. For a real guarantee of shape, a tool call or structured output is the mechanism. Prefill does not control length or tool selection."},

{ id:"hx-d4-03", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A prompt asks for 'a concise summary' and gets anything from two sentences to two pages. What is the defect?",
  a:1,
  opts:["The temperature is too high, since that produces variance in how much text is generated","The criterion is not decidable, because 'concise' means something different on each run","The output ceiling is too high, since that allows the two-page responses through","The input documents vary in length, since the summaries then vary in proportion"],
  why:"A vague criterion is the most common prompt defect: two readers would not agree on what 'concise' requires, and neither will the model between runs. Stating a checkable target — three bullets, under 80 words — removes the variance. Lowering the ceiling truncates rather than shortens."},

{ id:"hx-d4-04", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"You need the same extraction prompt to work across three languages. What is most likely to break?",
  a:3,
  opts:["The schema, which may not accommodate non-Latin characters in its field values","The token budget, since some languages require noticeably more tokens for the same content","The model's accuracy, which varies between the languages it was trained on","Few-shot examples written in one language, which bias the output language and format"],
  why:"Examples carry more than format: they implicitly demonstrate the language of the output. One-language examples pull multilingual input toward that language. Token density and accuracy do vary, but they degrade gracefully rather than breaking the contract."},

{ id:"hx-d4-05", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"When is a prefix worth caching?",
  multi:true, a:[0,2],
  opts:["It is long enough to exceed the minimum and is reused across many calls",
        "It changes on each request but the overall request size stays similar",
        "It sits at the front of the request and is byte-identical between calls",
        "The output it produces is structured and validated against a schema"],
  why:"Caching is a prefix match, so the stable material must come first, be long enough to qualify and be reused enough to repay the write premium. Content that changes per request cannot match, and the shape of the output is unrelated."},

{ id:"hx-d4-06", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An extraction returns a date as '03/04/2026' and downstream systems disagree about what it means. Where is the fix?",
  a:1,
  opts:["In the prompt, instructing the model to use a format such as ISO 8601","In the schema, constraining the field to a single explicit format such as ISO 8601","In the downstream systems, which should settle on one shared interpretation","In a post-processing step, which normalizes any date format after extraction"],
  why:"A format the schema can express should be enforced there, where it is checked rather than requested. A prompt instruction is advisory, downstream agreement does not fix the ambiguous value, and post-processing has to guess the original intent."},

{ id:"hx-d4-07", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A long prompt contains the task, a reference document, and output rules. What ordering serves both caching and quality?",
  a:2,
  opts:["Task first, then the rules, then the document, since the task sets the frame",
        "Document first, then the task and rules, so the material is read before the ask",
        "Stable rules and instructions first, the document next, then the specific task",
        "Rules last, immediately before generation, so they are freshest in context"],
  why:"Stable content first makes the prefix cacheable, and restating the specific task after a long document counters it being buried. Putting variable content first destroys the cache; putting rules alone at the end loses the cacheable prefix."},

{ id:"hx-d4-08", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What is the difference between structured output and a tool call for getting JSON?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Structured output constrains the response itself, while a tool call constrains an argument","Structured output is validated by the API while tool arguments are validated by your own code","Structured output supports nested objects while tool arguments must be flat","Structured output works on every model while tool calls require specific support"],
  why:"They constrain different things: structured output shapes the response the model returns, while a tool call shapes the arguments it passes to an operation. Both are schema-checked, both support nesting, and support varies for both."},

{ id:"hx-d4-09", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A summarization prompt occasionally invents a detail not present in the source. What reduces this most reliably?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Lowering the temperature so that the model stays closer to the wording of the input","Adding a firm instruction telling the model not to invent anything not in the source","Using a more capable model tier, which has better grounding behavior on this task","Requiring each claim to cite the passage it came from, then validating the citations"],
  why:"Requiring and then checking provenance turns an unverifiable claim into a checkable one — it is the only option that detects the problem rather than trying to discourage it. Temperature, instructions and tier all reduce the rate without giving you a way to catch what remains."},

{ id:"hx-d4-10", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Two prompts produce equally good output, but one costs three times more. What explains the difference most often?",
  a:1,
  opts:["The more expensive prompt requests a longer response from the model",
        "The more expensive prompt re-sends material that could be cached or omitted",
        "The more expensive prompt uses a higher temperature, which costs more to sample",
        "The more expensive prompt produces more reasoning before the visible answer"],
  why:"Input volume is where unnoticed cost accumulates: history, documents and tool definitions re-sent on every call. Output length and reasoning both cost, but a threefold gap with equal quality usually means something is being sent repeatedly. Temperature does not affect price."},

{ id:"hx-d4-11", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Why does XML-style tagging help with a prompt that mixes instructions and pasted content?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The API parses the tags and routes each section to a different part of the model",
        "Tagged sections are excluded from the token count, which reduces the cost",
        "The boundary makes clear where your instructions end and the data begins",
        "Tags are required whenever a prompt contains more than one kind of content"],
  why:"The value is the boundary: without it, a sentence inside pasted material can read as an instruction. The tags are not parsed by the API, are not free, and are a convention rather than a requirement."},

{ id:"hx-d4-12", scn:["extract"], exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Schema validation passes but a required field arrives as an empty string on 5% of calls. What should change?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Add a minimum-length constraint, so an empty value fails validation at the schema",
        "Add an instruction to the prompt stating that the field must never be empty",
        "Retry whenever the field is empty until a non-empty value is produced",
        "Treat the empty string as a legitimate absence and handle it downstream"],
  why:"If the schema can express the constraint, that is where it belongs — an empty string satisfying a 'string' type is exactly the gap. A prompt instruction is advisory, retrying masks a schema that permits the wrong value, and accepting it pushes the problem downstream."},

{ id:"hx-d4-13", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A prompt works well with three examples and worse with twelve. What is the likely cause?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Twelve examples exceed the number of patterns the model can attend to simultaneously","The additional examples introduced inconsistencies the model is now matching","The longer prompt pushes the task instruction beyond the context window","Examples have diminishing returns, so the extra nine simply added cost"],
  why:"More examples only help if they agree. Inconsistency between them teaches conflicting patterns, and quality can fall below what a smaller consistent set achieved. There is no hard limit on attention, and diminishing returns would plateau rather than degrade."},

{ id:"hx-d4-14", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d4", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What should a prompt's output specification state to be testable?",
  multi:true, a:[0,1,3],
  opts:["The structure expected, in a form that can be validated automatically",
        "What to do when the input does not support a complete answer",
        "The tone and register appropriate for the intended audience",
        "Concrete criteria for what counts as acceptable, not subjective adjectives"],
  why:"A validatable structure, defined edge-case behavior and checkable criteria can all be tested against a fixed set. Tone matters for quality but rarely has a decidable pass mark, so it does not make the specification testable."},

/* ---- CCAR-F D5: Context Management & Reliability (15%) ---- */

{ id:"hx-d5-01", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A conversation has reached 80% of the context window and the task is half done. What is the best next step?",
  a:1,
  opts:["Continue until the window is full, then start fresh with the user's original request",
        "Compact now: record the decisions and constraints reached, and drop the transcript",
        "Switch to a model with a larger window and resend the conversation unchanged",
        "Remove the oldest messages until there is room, keeping the recent exchanges"],
  why:"Compacting before saturation preserves the conclusions while there is still room to act on them. Waiting until the window is full loses the ability to summarize well, a larger window defers the same problem at higher cost, and dropping the oldest messages discards the framing that is often most important."},

{ id:"hx-d5-02", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What should be true of anything a long-running agent writes to external storage?",
  a:2,
  opts:["It should be written only at the end, so partial state is never persisted",
        "It should be encrypted, since external storage is outside the trust boundary",
        "It should be enough to resume from, including which side effects already fired",
        "It should be a complete copy of the conversation, so nothing is lost on restart"],
  why:"The purpose of externalized state is resumption, and the dangerous gap is not knowing which irreversible actions already happened. Writing only at the end defeats the purpose, a full transcript copy is usually unnecessary, and encryption is a separate concern."},

{ id:"hx-d5-03", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A retry storm takes down a downstream service after a brief outage. What was missing from the client?",
  a:3,
  opts:["A cap on the number of retries each individual request was permitted",
        "A longer initial timeout, so fewer requests were considered failed",
        "A dead-letter path for requests that could not be completed",
        "Backoff with jitter, so recovering clients did not retry in lockstep"],
  why:"Synchronized retries are what turn an outage into a second outage: every client waits the same interval and hits the service together. Jitter spreads them. A retry cap limits one client's contribution, and the other options do not address the simultaneity."},

{ id:"hx-d5-04", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent summarizes its own context when space runs low, and quality degrades over several compactions. Why?",
  a:0,
  opts:["Each summary summarizes the previous one, so detail is lost compounding each time","Summarization is using a cheaper model tier than the main conversation does","The summaries are appended to the history rather than replacing what they cover","Compaction discards the system prompt along with the conversation history itself"],
  why:"Repeatedly summarizing a summary loses a little each round and the loss accumulates. Compacting from an authoritative record rather than from the previous summary avoids it. The other options describe implementation mistakes that would show immediately rather than degrading gradually."},

{ id:"hx-d5-05", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Which signals should be logged to make a production agent diagnosable?",
  multi:true, a:[0,1,3],
  opts:["The request ID, so a specific call can be traced with support",
        "The stop reason, which distinguishes completion from truncation",
        "The full text of every message, retained indefinitely for later analysis",
        "Token usage, which attributes cost and reveals cache effectiveness"],
  why:"Request ID, stop reason and token usage are compact and each answers a question you will actually ask. Retaining full message text indefinitely is a privacy and retention liability rather than an observability requirement."},

{ id:"hx-d5-06", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"A user reports that the agent 'forgot' a constraint they set twenty turns ago. What does a good design do about this?",
  a:2,
  opts:["Increase the context window so twenty turns comfortably fit within it",
        "Instruct the model to re-read the conversation before each response",
        "Hold constraints outside the transcript and re-supply them on every turn",
        "Reduce the verbosity of tool results so more conversation stays in context"],
  why:"A constraint that must hold for the whole session cannot depend on surviving compaction. Extracting it into state the system re-injects makes it reliable. A bigger window and leaner tool results delay the problem, and re-reading does not help once the content is gone."},

{ id:"hx-d5-07", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"Latency is acceptable at the median but terrible at the 99th percentile. What should you examine?",
  a:1,
  opts:["The median path — the common case, the bulk of the traffic, the usual request","What the slow requests have in common — input size, retries, or a particular tool","The model tier — its typical latency, its variance, and its published figures","Total throughput — the request rate, the queue depth, the headroom remaining"],
  why:"A long tail has a cause the median does not share, so the diagnosis starts with what those specific requests have in common. Optimizing the median leaves the tail untouched, and tier variability would shift the whole distribution rather than only its end."},

{ id:"hx-d5-08", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"Why does a 529 warrant a longer backoff than a 429?",
  a:0,
  opts:["A 429 is your own quota, while a 529 means the service as a whole is under strain","A 529 is a permanent failure while a 429 clears once the window resets","A 429 includes a retry hint in the response while a 529 does not","A 529 indicates a malformed request that will fail again if retried quickly"],
  why:"A rate limit is specific to your usage and clears predictably; an overload is system-wide, so retrying aggressively adds to the problem. Both are retryable, and neither indicates a malformed request — that would be a 400."},

{ id:"hx-d5-09", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"An agent's tool results are verbose and the conversation saturates after six turns. What is the structural fix?",
  a:3,
  opts:["Compact the conversation more often, keeping the window from filling up","Move to a model with a larger window, letting six turns fit comfortably","Reduce the turn count, combining several requests into a single exchange","Have the tools return what the model needs, not everything the source provides"],
  why:"The cause is at the source, so shaping tool output fixes the rate at which context is consumed. Compaction and a larger window treat the symptom and still pay for the tokens, and combining turns does not reduce the volume returned."},

{ id:"hx-d5-10", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"scenario",
  q:"You need to reproduce a user's reported failure from three days ago. What must have been captured?",
  a:2,
  opts:["The user's own description — what they expected the agent to do instead","Aggregate metrics — the figures covering the period the failure fell in","The exact request — messages, parameters and model version — for that run","An error log — every error the system emitted across that day's traffic"],
  why:"Reproduction needs the precise inputs and configuration of the run, since behavior depends on all of them. Expectations, aggregates and error logs tell you something went wrong without letting you re-create it."},

{ id:"hx-d5-11", exam:"CCAR-F", d:"d5", heldOut:true, kind:"recall",
  q:"What distinguishes a transient failure from one that will not clear on retry?",
  a:1,
  opts:["A transient failure returns a 5xx status; a permanent one returns a 4xx instead","A transient failure is caused by conditions that change; a permanent one by the request itself","A transient failure carries a retry-after header; a permanent one does not","A transient failure happens intermittently; a permanent one happens every time"],
  why:"The distinction is whether the cause lies in the request or its environment: a malformed request fails identically forever, while an overload clears. The status-code correlation is a useful heuristic but imperfect — a 429 is a 4xx and is retryable."}

);
